{
  "teamId": 144,
  "year": 2019,
  "narrative": "The 2019 Atlanta Braves arrived with expectations after their surprising NL East title a year prior, but the early going was anything but smooth. They stumbled out of March without a win in three games, then played inconsistent baseball through April, going 14-12 while still searching for an identity. The bullpen, particularly Luke Jackson and A.J. Minter, flashed the vulnerabilities that would haunt stretches of the season, but the offense — led by a rejuvenated Josh Donaldson and a maturing Ronald Acuña Jr. — provided enough firepower to keep the Braves afloat.\n\nEverything changed in June. The Braves went a scorching 20-8, the best month of their season, propelled by clutch hits from unlikely heroes like Brian McCann and rookie Austin Riley, who burst onto the scene with back-to-back home runs and looked like the next cornerstone of the franchise. That summer surge carried enormous momentum into July and August, when the team kept winning despite ongoing bullpen hiccups. Acuña was electric, Rafael Ortega delivered a stunning grand slam out of nowhere, and even on the nights the relievers faltered, the Braves found ways to answer. By the time August wound down, Atlanta had accumulated 97 wins and a commanding grip on the NL East.\n\nThe final days of September offered a fitting encapsulation of the Braves' season — Adeiny Hechavarría, of all people, smashed two home runs in the same game, only for Grant Dayton to surrender a crushing three-run blast that turned a win into a loss. It was a season-in-miniature: punch, counterpunch, resilience, and vulnerability all in one evening. The Braves had earned their division title on merit, but the demons in the bullpen were never fully exorcised.\n\nOctober brought the St. Louis Cardinals and a swift, painful reckoning. Mark Melancon's struggles in Game 1 opened the door for St. Louis to seize momentum, and though the Braves fought back admirably in Game 2 — Adam Duvall's clutch single, Dansby Swanson's gap shot, and Ozzie Albies' postseason home run flashing the team's ceiling — Shane Greene's wobble in Game 3 proved costly. The Cardinals closed out the series, ending Atlanta's season short of where this talented group hoped to go. The 97-win Braves were a genuinely excellent team, but October reminded everyone that regular-season excellence only earns you a chance, never a guarantee.",
